Windows 365 uses both default and custom OS images to automatically create virtual Cloud PCs that you provide to your end users. The default images are available from the gallery in Microsoft Intune as a part of creating your provisioning policy. You can also upload custom images(opens in a new tab) that you create.
 
 
o	Both marketplace and custom images must meet the following requirements:
o	Supported versions of Windows 10 or Windows 11 Enterprise
o	Generation 2 images
o	Images must never have been AD and Microsoft Entra ID joined and Intune-enrolled or enrolled for co-management (For more information, refer to Sysprep won't run correctly on a device that has been MDM enrolled(opens in a new tab).)
o	Generalized VM image
o	Single session VM images (multi-session isn’t supported)
o	No recovery partition (For information about how to remove a recovery partition, refer to Windows Server command: delete partition(opens in a new tab).)
o	Default 64 GB OS disk size (The OS disk size is automatically adjusted to the size specified in SKU description of the Windows 365 license.)
 
Gallery Images 
 
Windows 365 provides a built-in gallery of Windows Enterprise images accessible through the provisioning policy creation flow. Each image helps admins with preset audit policies already enabled, like account policies, logon or logoff, object access, and policy change.
There are two sets of images available to choose from across the different versions of Windows Enterprise:
•	Images with pre-installed Microsoft 365 Apps
•	Images with no preinstalled applications
 
Custom Image
 
If none of the default gallery images meet your requirements, you can upload up to 20 of your own custom device images. For more information on creating a custom image, refer to Create a managed image of a generalized VM in Azure(opens in a new tab).
A custom image can be created using any of the images mentioned previously as a starting point(opens in a new tab).
 
 
During Cloud PC provisioning, the state indicates the current status of the Cloud PC in the setup process. Here are the main states you might encounter:
•	Cloud PC provisioning states
•	Equivalent states
•	Transient states

Windows 365 deployment overview 
After an organization has purchased one or more Windows 365 licenses, the Windows 365 node in Microsoft Intune will become active for management. At this point, you can provision Cloud PCs.
To set up a system to provision on-demand Cloud PCs to users, follow these steps:
1.	Assign licenses to users(opens in a new tab).
2.	Create Azure network connection(opens in a new tab) (optional).
3.	Provide localized Windows experience(opens in a new tab) (optional).
4.	Add device images(opens in a new tab) (optional).
5.	Create a provisioning policy(opens in a new tab).

 
 
 
Cloud PC Provisioning states 
 
The provisioning states for Cloud PCs in Windows 365 include:
•	None: No record in the database or it's deleted
•	Licensed: User is licensed but no policy is assigned
•	Provisioned: Device is provisioned and available for use
•	InGracePeriod Reprovision: Device will be reprovisioned after the grace period expires
•	InGracePeriod Deprovision: Device will be deprovisioned after the grace period expires
•	InGracePeriod Delete: Device will be deleted after the grace period expires
•	Pending: Workspace will move to the Provisioning state when quota is available
 
Equivalent States
 
Equivalent states refer to the different statuses a Cloud PC can have during its lifecycle.
 
•	Deprovisioned: Licensed but the last state was Deprovisioning
•	Deprovision Failed: Provisioned but the last state was Deprovisioned (not consistent with the license policy configuration)
•	Upgraded: Provisioned but the last state was Upgrading
•	Upgrade Failed: Provisioned but the last state was Upgrading (not consistent with the license policy configuration)
•	Provision Failed: Licensed but the last state was Provisioning (not consistent with the license policy configuration)
•	Reprovision Failed: Provisioned but the last two states were Reprovision and Provisioning (last action failed but consistent with the license policy configuration)
•	Restored: Provisioned but the last state was Restoring
•	Restore Failed: Provisioned but the last state was Restoring (last action failed but consistent with the license policy configuration)
 
Transient states
 
Transient states are temporary statuses that a Cloud PC might go through during provisioning or other operations.
•	Provisioning: From Licensed to Provisioned
•	Upgrading: From Provisioned to Provisioned triggered by the upgrade process
•	Deprovisioning: From InGracePeriod Deprovision to Deprovisioned or from InGracePeriod Delete to None
•	Restoring: From Provisioned to Provisioned triggered by the restore action
 
 
 
Grace Period 
 
When a license or assignment change occurs for a user with a current Cloud PC, the Cloud PC object is marked as InGracePeriod. Grace periods help admins avoid licensing or targeting mistakes that might cause users to lose access to their Cloud PC.
When a Cloud PC enters a grace period, an alert is triggered.
 
In Windows 365 Enterprise and Frontline, the grace period for Cloud PCs is generally seven days. The grace period in Windows 365 Business is only 24 hours. During this period, users can continue to access their Cloud PCs. After the grace period ends, users are logged off, access is lost, and the Cloud PC is deprovisioned.
Let's check out the grace period for each scenario.
 
Grace period scenario	Length
Reprovision	Enterprise (7 days), Business (1 day)
Deprovision	Enterprise (7 days), Business (1 day)
Delete	Enterprise (7 days), Business (1 day)
Fraud Block	Enterprise (7 days), Business (3 days if used and 1 day if never used)
Guard Rail	15 days (in the don't touch list)
 
 
 
Resize a Cloud PC
 
The Resize remote action allows you to upgrade the Random Access Memory (RAM), Central Processing Unit (CPU), and storage size of a Cloud PC, or downgrade the RAM and CPU, while preserving user and disk data without requiring reprovisioning. This is useful when a user needs higher RAM and CPU for intensive applications, more disk space, or less RAM and CPU for current workloads.
 
 
Requirements for Resize
 
Role Requirements 
 
To resize a Cloud PC, the admin must have certain built-in Microsoft Entra roles.
For direct assigned Cloud PC provisioning
•	Intune Service Administrator
•	Intune Reader and Cloud PC Admin roles
•	Intune Reader and Windows 365 Administrator roles
For group-based Cloud PC provisioning
•	Intune Service Administrator
•	Intune Reader and Windows 365 Administrator roles
•	Microsoft Entra group read or write membership and licensing permissions.
 
 
Ipaddress Requirements 
 
When resizing a Microsoft Entra hybrid join bring-your-own-network Cloud PC, a second IP address must be available in the subnet for the Cloud PC to be resized.
During the resizing operation, a second IP address is used when moving to the new size. This precaution makes sure that the Cloud PC can be rolled back to the original should an issue occur.
 
 
Other Requirements
 
In order to use the Resize option, there must be available licenses in inventory for the resized Cloud PC configuration. To resize a Cloud PC, it must have a status of Provisioned in the Windows 365 provisioning node.
 
 
 
 
Restoring Cloud PC
 
Point-in-time restore lets an administrator restore a Cloud PC to the exact state it was at an earlier point in time. You can create restore points at regular intervals or on-demand restore points for specific times. Admins can also give users permission to restore their own Cloud PCs.
 
 
Short-term restore points
 
Set 10 short-term restore points every 4, 6, 12, 16, or 24 hours.
 
Long-term restore points
 
There are four non-configurable restore points that are saved every seven days.
 
On-demand manual restore points
 
Administrators can create a manual restore point, and each Cloud PC can have only one manual restore point at a time.


